    "title": "Fluphenazine Decanoate, Oral Fluphenazine, and Placebo in Treatment of Remitted Schizophrenics II. Rating Scale Data",
    "authors": "Arthur Rifkin, MD; Frederic Quitkin, MD; Donald F. Klein, MD",
    "year": "1977",
    "journal": "Archives of General Psychiatry",
    "country": "United States",
    "doi": null,
    "study_design": "Randomized, double-blind, controlled trial. Randomization method not specified.",
    "inclusion_criteria": "All patients referred to the clinic with a final hospital diagnosis of schizophrenia, any subtype, were eligible for the study. The patients were rediagnosed in the aftercare clinic. The diagnosis of schizophrenia was based on Kraepelinian criteria such as delusions, hallucinations, obviously disorganized speech, and bizarre behavior, in the absence of criteria for an organic syndrome or mood disorder. The patient entered the study if (1) he reached a stable remission from fluphenazine decanoate and oral fluphenazine for at least four weeks; (2) he had no more than minor side effects; (3) he was cooperative in keeping appointments for therapy and injections (but he need not have been considered a reliable pill-taker); and (4) he gave informed consent.",
    "exclusion_criteria": "Patients who did not regularly keep their appointments during the baseline period were excluded.",
    "study_setting": "Aftercare Clinic of the Long Island Jewish-Hillside Medical Center",
    "source_of_recruitment": "All were referred from the inpatient service or the day hospital.",
    "number_of_participants_randomized": "73",
    "number_of_participants_analyzed": "73",
    "dropout_reasons": "Patients were terminated because of relapse, toxic effects, or dropout.",
